Brute Force:

class Solution {
    public ArrayList<Integer> rangeSumQueries(int[] arr, int[][] queries) {
        // code here
        ArrayList<Integer> result=new ArrayList<>();
        for(int i=0;i<queries.length;i++){
            int left=queries[i][0];
            int right=queries[i][1];
            int sum=0;
            for(int j=left;j<=right;j++){
                sum+=arr[j];
            }
            result.add(sum);
        }
        return result;
        
    }
}

Optimal Approach:

class Solution {
    public ArrayList<Integer> rangeSumQueries(int[] arr, int[][] queries) {
        // code here
        ArrayList<Integer> result=new ArrayList<>();
        int [] prefix=new int[arr.length];
        prefix[0]=arr[0];
        for(int i=1;i<arr.length;i++){
            prefix[i]=prefix[i-1]+ arr[i];
        }
        for(int i=0;i<queries.length;i++){
            int left=queries[i][0];
            int right=queries[i][1];
            if(left==0){
                result.add(prefix[right]);
            }else{
                result.add(prefix[right] - prefix[left - 1]);
            }
        }
        return result;
        
    }
}

Interview Explanation:
I first considered the brute-force approach, where I calculate the sum for each query by iterating from L to R. That would take O(n) per query, giving O(n × q) overall, which is inefficient for large inputs.

Since the array is immutable and there are no updates, I preprocess it using a prefix sum array. Each prefix[i] stores the cumulative sum from index 0 to i.

For each query, if L is 0, the answer is simply prefix[R]. Otherwise, I subtract the cumulative sum before L, so the answer is prefix[R] - prefix[L-1]. This lets me answer every query in O(1) time.

Building the prefix array takes O(n), processing all queries takes O(q), so the overall time complexity is O(n + q), and the space complexity is O(n)
